"hadiths[6]{hadithnumber,text}:"
1380,"Yaqoub bin Ibrahim Al-Dawraqi, Ibn Ulayya told us, on the authority of Hisham Al-Dastawi, Abu Al-Zubair told us, on the authority of Jabir bin Abdullah, He said: The sun eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, on a very hot day, so he led his companions in prayer, and they prolonged the prayer until they made They fell down, then he bowed and prolonged, then he rose and prolonged, then he prostrated two prostrations, then he rose and did something like that, so it was four rak’ahs. And four prostrations, then he said: “Indeed, everything you were promised to me has been presented to me,” so he mentioned the hadith in its length, and said: “And they used to say: The sun And the moon does not eclipse except due to a great death, and they are two of the signs of God that He shows you. So when they are eclipsed, pray until they are cleared.”"
1381,"Bandar told us, Abd al-Ala told us, Hisham told us, on the authority of Abu al-Zubayr, on the authority of Jabir, who said: The sun was eclipsed during the reign of the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace. On a very hot day, the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, led his companions in prayer. He prolonged standing until they made them fall down, then he bowed down. So he prolonged it, then he got up and did the same thing, then he made it move forward and then move back, so it was four rak’ahs and four prostrations, then he said: “It is I was offered everything that you are promised, so Paradise was offered to me until I took part of it, and if I wanted, I could have taken it. Then I took part of it but fell short. my hand away from him, then fire was shown to me, so I kept back for fear that it would engulf you, and I saw in it a tall, black donkey woman being tortured by a cat. She tied it up, did not feed it, and did not let it eat of the vermin of the land, and I saw Abu Thumama Amr bin Malik dragging his reed through the fire. And they used to say: The sun and the moon do not eclipse except due to a great death, and they are two signs of God that God shows you. So when they eclipse So pray until you are saved.” Bundar did not tell us: “the moon.” And in the report of Ata’ bin Yasar, on the authority of Ibn Abbas, and Kathir bin Abbas, on the authority of Ibn Abbas and Urwa, And Amra, on the authority of Aisha: “He bowed two bows in each rak’ah.”"
1382,"Bundar narrated to us, Muadh bin Hisham narrated to us, on the authority of Abi, and Ibn Abi Adi, on the authority of Hisham, on the authority of Qatada, on the authority of Ata, on the authority of Ubaid bin Umair, on the authority of Aisha, that the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, “prayed six rak’ahs and four prostrations during an eclipse.”"
1383,"Yaqoub bin Ibrahim told us, Ibn Ulayya told us, Ibn Jurayj told us, on the authority of Ata’, h, and Muhammad bin Hisham told us, he told us Ismail means Ibn Ulayya. Ibn Jurayj told us on the authority of Ata’. He said: I heard Ubaid bin Umair speaking. He said: Tell me whom to believe. He said: I thought he meant Aisha, may God be pleased with her, because she said: The sun was eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, so he stood up. He stood with the people, then he bowed, then he stood, then he bowed, so he bowed two rak’ahs, three rak’ahs in each rak’ah, so he bowed the third. Then he prostrated, to the point that some men would faint on that day, until a pool of water would be poured on them for what they had done to them. He would say when he said the takbir: “Allahu Akbar.” Then, when he raised his head, he said: “God hears those who praise Him,” and he did not leave until the sun rose. Then he got up and thanked God and praised Him, and said: “The sun And the moon does not eclipse for the death or life of anyone, but they are two of the signs of God with which He will frighten you. So when it is eclipsed, fear God until ""Yenjalia"""
1384,"And in the report of Abd al-Malik, on the authority of Ata’, on the authority of Jabir: “Six rak’ahs in four prostrations.”"
1385,"Abu Musa told us, Yahya told us, on the authority of Sufyan, Habib told us, on the authority of Tawus, on the authority of Ibn Abbas, on the authority of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace. He said: “He prayed during a eclipse, so he recited, then bowed, then read, then bowed, then recited, then bowed, then recited, then bowed, then prostrated.” And the other is like it.” Abu Bakr said: I have mentioned the methods of these reports in the Book of Al-Kabir, so it is permissible for a person to pray during the eclipse however he likes, and whatever he wishes. The Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, performed the number of bows. If he liked, he bowed two rak’ahs in each rak’ah, and if he liked, he performed three rak’ahs in each rak’ah, and if he liked He prayed four rak'ahs in each rak'ah. Because all of these reports are authentic on the authority of the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, and these reports indicate that the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, prayed during an eclipse. The sun several times, not just once"
